{"name":"Bevelled Glass","hex":"#7accb8","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bevelled","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of bevel"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a bevel, especially at an edge"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bevelled"},{"term":"glass","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An amorphous solid, often transparent substance, usually made by melting silica sand with various additives (for most purposes, a mixture of soda, potash and lime is added).","Any amorphous solid (one without a regular crystal lattice)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To fit with glass; to glaze.","To enclose in glass."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glass"}],"entries":[{"title":"Beveled glass","blocks":[{"text":"Beveled glass is usually made by taking thick glass and creating an angled surface cut (bevel) around the entire periphery. Bevels act as prisms in sunlight creating an interesting color refraction which both highlights the glass work and provides a spectrum of colors which would ordinarily be absent in clear float glass."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Mirror%2C_ca._1910_%28CH_52027753%29.jpg/330px-Mirror%2C_ca._1910_%28CH_52027753%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beveled_glass","wikidata":"Q4899357"},{"title":"Glass","blocks":[{"text":"Glass is an amorphous (non-crystalline) solid. Because it is often transparent and chemically inert, glass has found widespread practical, technological, and decorative use in window panes, tableware, and optics. Some common objects made of glass are named after the material, e.g., a \"glass\" for drinking, \"glasses\" for vision correction, and a \"magnifying glass\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Fassade_Wilhelmstrasse_65%2C_Berlin-Mitte%2C_160417%2C_ako.jpg/3